    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me focus on the actual information.\n\nFor checked luggage, you can weigh your bags at a designated weighbridge or a self-service weighbridge at the station. If your bags exceed the weight limit, you'll need to check them in at a designated check-in desk. You can check in multiple bags, but it's recommended to check them in at a desk to avoid any issues.\n\nAs for taking your bags through the gates, you can do so, but you'll need to ensure they fit in the designated luggage racks on the train. If you have multiple bags, you can take them through the gates yourself, but be aware that you'll need to collect them at the other end of the journey.\n\nRegarding navigating the station, to avoid getting lost, I was saying that you can use the Eurostar website or a station map to plan your route. The station is quite large, so it's easy to get disoriented. You can also ask a station staff member for directions if you're unsure. Just be aware that the station can get quite busy, especially during peak travel times, so be prepared to navigate through crowds.",
